Adds to the survey report (no code change):

* Per-batch results table for the tests/python/test_experiment_*.py sweep as it lands. Batches 00-02
  (750 of 3,366 files) move 25 tracked files.
* Batch 01's 16 include the two NON-results/ files from the original hazard
  (openspec/papers/paper-v6/section-6-limitations.md, output/kanele_synth/post_synth.dcp) AND one the
  hazard commit did not list at all: README.md, which CLAUDE.md's Public Documentation Discipline
  marks operator-curated. Two of the 16 are 2026-07-27 incident artifacts (see the report's §3.5).
* §3.6, recorded because it is instructive rather than embarrassing: a background collector built to
  measure the hardcoded-path class called `--check --restore` on a poll loop and reverted an
  in-progress edit to the report itself. A file the author is editing is indistinguishable, at the
  git layer, from a file a test rewrote. The edit was reconstructed and the collector killed by
  explicit PID. This is exactly why the shipped tool makes --check read-only and --restore opt-in,
  with test_the_detector_never_edits_anything_unless_asked pinning it. Never run --restore on a
  schedule against a tree someone is working in.

Verified after the incident: determination-preservation-lint --all is clean across every tracked
results/*.json, and the two artifacts that leaked into the canonical repo during the survey
(experiment_2427_kv260_yosys_v4.json, and earlier experiment_3734) were restored byte-identically.

